  "body": "  What does the computation above tell us?      Total production never reaches on this range, so always stays on its first piece. We can use and get , exactly as in the first model.    Correct, and this is the payoff for pinning down Firm 2's best response first. Once , total production is capped at , so the second piece of is unreachable and the piecewise complication never actually bites.      The price stays positive, but we still have to maximize the second piece of separately and compare.    The second piece applies only when , and the computation shows for every in this range. There's no left for that piece to describe, so there's nothing to compare against.      Firm 2 always produces less than Firm 1.    That happens to be false at small (at , Firm 2 produces ), and in any case it isn't what the computation is about. The quantity being bounded is the total  , which is what decides which piece of is in force.      It tells us directly that .    Not yet. This only establishes which formula to maximize. Finding still takes a derivative, which is the next step.     "
