  "body": "  You've now found the Nash equilibria of two games by inspection. The Prisoner's Dilemma had one, , paying , even though both executives would rather have . Stag Hunt had two, and both hunters prefer to . Taken together, what do these two results tell you about Nash equilibria?      A Nash equilibrium may or may not be good for the players. It is a profile the game can get stuck at, because no player can improve their own payoff by changing strategy alone.    Correct, and this is the one statement that covers both games at once. Stuck is the right word: the definition says nothing about whether an equilibrium is a good outcome, only that no single player can walk away from it and do better.      A Nash equilibrium is the outcome that is best for all the players.    The Prisoner's Dilemma rules this out. Both executives would rather have than the equilibrium payoff , and yet the equilibrium is where the game gets stuck.      A Nash equilibrium is an outcome that no player would rather trade for a different one.    Again the Prisoner's Dilemma is the counterexample. Both executives would happily trade for . What they cannot do is get there by changing strategy on their own, and that's the only thing the definition asks about.     "
