  "body": "  Chicken, Battle of the Sexes, and Stag Hunt all have two Nash equilibria. What separates the first two games from Stag Hunt?      The equilibria in Chicken and Battle of the Sexes are not strict, while the ones in Stag Hunt are.    All of these equilibria are strict. In Battle of the Sexes, for instance, if Alice leaves the concert while Bob stays there, her payoff drops from to , a strict decrease. Strictness isn't what distinguishes these games.      In Stag Hunt both players prefer the same one of the two equilibria, while in Chicken and Battle of the Sexes each player prefers a different one.    Correct. In Stag Hunt the players agree about which equilibrium they want and the difficulty is getting there together. In Chicken and Battle of the Sexes they disagree, so there's a genuine conflict over which equilibrium should happen. That's why Stag Hunt is called a pure coordination game and the other two are not.      In Chicken and Battle of the Sexes, a player can reach their preferred equilibrium by changing strategy on their own.    If that were true, the profile they started at would not have been a Nash equilibrium in the first place. That's exactly what the definition rules out. Getting from one equilibrium to the other requires both players to move.     "
